                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Map the battlefield: globalised pressure and transferable assets`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Continental has signed a national modern-trade deal, committed <strong>Rp1,9 triliun</strong> over 18 months and priced 12% below you. Before you spend a rupiah, apply Frost's two axes to each category: how <strong>globalised</strong> is the competitive pressure, and how <strong>transferable</strong> are your local assets to a global player? Your 480.000-warung network and local product fit are <em>non-transferable</em> — the giant cannot replicate last-mile trust, knowledge of Indonesian water and hair, or a cost base built for warung price points. Name honestly where you are exposed (premium, modern trade) and where you are decisive (the mass core through the warungs).`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the posture and defend the core`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Commit a posture across the portfolio: <strong>Defend</strong> the warung-strong core, <strong>Dodge</strong> the premium tier into value/niche space the giant won't chase, or contend head-on. Then set the laundry <strong>pricing</strong> response. The <em>price-war trap</em>: matching Continental's 12% undercut collapses EBITDA from 13% toward 6% — fighting on scale, the one battlefield where a USD 70B rival is strongest and you are weakest. Choose your <strong>weapon</strong>: the warung network and local R&D the giant cannot copy, or a TV-and-modern-trade push you lose 6-to-1. State explicitly which battles you will <em>not</em> fight.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Allocate scarce capital and make the hard trade-off`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Split <strong>Rp350 miliar</strong> across protecting warung distribution, local R&D, a modern-trade beachhead and retaining the regional sales managers Continental is raiding at <strong>+40%</strong>. You cannot fund everything — spreading thin loses everywhere against a Rp1,9 triliun war chest. Mid-round, Continental's relationship manager privately offers a <strong>co-distribution partnership</strong>: its brands through your warungs for a margin share. Lucrative and de-risking — but it puts the rival's brands into all 480.000 warungs, on <em>your</em> relationships. In three years, whose network is it?`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — 18-month outcome and the survival strategy`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The market resolves: your share trajectory, margin and moat depend on the postures and bets you made. Now pitch the family-owner board a <strong>survival-and-growth strategy</strong>. Do you extend the warung playbook into adjacent categories and neighbouring ASEAN markets where it travels, chase Continental into premium modern trade, or freeze on pure defence? Do you <strong>institutionalise</strong> local advantage so the next giant is met from strength, or rely on heroics? The board wants the headline — give them an honest survival case, not a price-war victory lap that hides a collapsing margin.`,

