                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_pressure_right: `<strong>Right read.</strong> Global pressure concentrates in modern trade and the premium tier, where brands, R&D and a 6-to-1 budget dominate. That tells you where to <em>dodge</em>, not defend — and where your scarce capital is wasted fighting the giant's strengths.`,
                    coach_r1_everywhere:    `<strong>"Unbeatable everywhere" is paralysis dressed as realism.</strong> A USD 70B rival is fearsome in modern trade and on TV — but it has no warungs, no Indonesian water-and-hair know-how, and no cost base for warung price points. Find the ground where <em>you</em> are unbeatable.`,
                    coach_r1_warung:        `<strong>The warungs are where you are strongest, not weakest.</strong> No multinational has ever replicated 480.000 last-mile relationships, weekly motorbike visits and informal credit. Pressure is global where scale wins — not in your own kiosks.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>This is how a #1 becomes an also-ran.</strong> A top-three global player with a Rp1,9 triliun launch is not bluffing. Underestimating the entry is exactly the complacency that loses 8–12 share points before you react.`,
                    coach_r1_map_sharp:     `<strong>Sharp map.</strong> You located global pressure in modern trade AND named the warung network as your non-transferable moat. That is Frost's diagnosis done right: defend where your assets are decisive, dodge where the giant's scale is.`,
                    coach_r1_brand:         `<strong>Out-shouting them on TV is the giant's game.</strong> Brand advertising is the most transferable, most outspent battlefield there is — they beat you 6-to-1. Your defensible asset is the warung network they cannot buy, not airtime.`,
                    coach_r1_balance:       `<strong>You cannot match a USD 70B balance sheet.</strong> Believing you can gear up to their spend is the fastest route to a price war you lose. Your advantage is non-transferable distribution, not capital.`,
                    coach_r1_rnd:           `<strong>R&D scale alone won't save you</strong> — Frost's own research shows MNEs increasingly localise innovation. But the warung relationships and deep local fit stay locked to you. Concentrate on the advantage the giant cannot localise.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>Refusing to concede any ground is its own trap.</strong> If you won't honestly name where the giant will win (premium, modern trade), you'll defend everywhere and lose everywhere. Choosing where <em>not</em> to fight concentrates force.`,
                    coach_r2_defenddodge:   `<strong>Defend-and-dodge is the textbook local-champion posture.</strong> Fortify the warung-strong mass core where you are decisive, and sidestep the premium tier into value space the giant won't chase. You meet force with focus, not with a thin line everywhere.`,
                    coach_r2_defendall:     `<strong>Defending every SKU at full strength spreads Rp350 miliar against Rp1,9 triliun.</strong> Thin enough to lose everywhere. Frost's grid asks the harder question: where are your assets transferable and decisive, and where should you dodge?`,
                    coach_r2_globalcontend: `<strong>Going on the offensive plays to their strengths.</strong> Out-marketing and out-innovating a USD 70B rival with a fraction of its budget is the surest way to burn capital and margin. Contend selectively; defend the moat.`,
                    coach_r2_retreat:       `<strong>Conceding the core is surrender, not strategy.</strong> You're abandoning the warung-strong categories where the giant is weakest — handing it the very ground you could hold. Defend where you are decisive before you harvest.`,
                    coach_r2_value:         `<strong>Holding price and competing on local value is the winning move.</strong> Trust, access and Indonesian fit beat a headline 12% undercut — and you keep the 13% margin a price war would erase. You refused the giant's battlefield.`,
                    coach_r2_match:         `<strong>You've matched their 12% cut. EBITDA just fell from 13% toward 6% — roughly Rp290 miliar of profit gone.</strong> Continental can sustain a loss in Indonesia for a decade; can your balance sheet? You are fighting where they are strongest and you are weakest.`,
                    coach_r2_tvmodern:      `<strong>A TV-and-modern-trade push is the marketer's game — and they outspend you 6-to-1.</strong> Meanwhile you've under-funded the one thing they cannot buy: 480.000 warung owners who trust your reps. Why fight on their turf instead of yours?`,
                    coach_r2_discount:      `<strong>Deep discounting to hold shelf is a slow price war.</strong> It bleeds margin without building the moat, and the giant can always discount deeper. Compete on value and access, not on who can give product away longest.`,
                    coach_r3_concentrate:   `<strong>Force concentrated on the moat.</strong> Heavy funding of the warung network plus protecting at-risk talent backs the non-transferable advantage the giant cannot replicate. This is how scarce capital beats a war chest — by being decisive somewhere.`,
                    coach_r3_abandonmoat:   `<strong>You've under-funded the moat to chase modern trade.</strong> Starving warung distribution while betting on the giant's fastest channel is fighting on their turf. The 480.000 warungs are your one durable advantage — fund them first.`,
                    coach_r3_spread:        `<strong>Capital is spread evenly — and thinly.</strong> Against a Rp1,9 triliun war chest, an even split loses everywhere. Concentrate on warung distribution and the people who carry it; accept you cannot fund the giant's channels too.`,
                    coach_r3_talent:        `<strong>You forgot the talent raid.</strong> Continental is poaching your best regional managers at +40%, and they leave with warung relationships. The moat is made of people — under-fund retention and the moat walks out the door.`,
                    coach_r3_decline:       `<strong>Declining the partnership protects the moat.</strong> Lucrative as it looked, putting the rival's brands through your warungs would have monetised the one asset that makes you uncopyable. You kept the key to your own network.`,
                    coach_r3_firewall:      `<strong>A ring-fenced deal is a defensible compromise.</strong> Non-competing categories only, your reps, time-boxed — you take some revenue without arming Continental inside your core. The discipline is in the terms, not the yes/no.`,
                    coach_r3_open:          `<strong>You've handed the enemy the key to your moat.</strong> Continental's brands now flow through all 480.000 warungs, on your relationships. The margin looks great this year — but in three years, whose network is it? You monetised the one thing that made you valuable.`,
                    coach_r4_adjacent:      `<strong>Extending the playbook is growth from strength.</strong> The warung model and local fit travel to adjacent categories and to neighbouring ASEAN markets where the same dynamics hold. You turn a defensive win into a durable franchise the next giant also can't easily enter.`,
                    coach_r4_headon:        `<strong>Chasing Continental into premium modern trade is the giant's home ground.</strong> You'd be out-spent 6-to-1 again, bleeding margin in the one channel you can't win. Grow where your moat travels, not where their scale does.`,
                    coach_r4_defensive:     `<strong>Pure defence with no growth slowly cedes the initiative.</strong> Holding the core is necessary but a static incumbent eventually loses to a giant that keeps moving. Extend the advantage while it is strong.`,
                    coach_r4_heroics:       `<strong>Relying on family heroics doesn't survive a generation.</strong> Personal relationships are real, but if they aren't embedded in systems, succession and rep retention, the moat erodes the day a key person leaves. Institutionalise it.`,
                    coach_r4_nothing:       `<strong>"The win will protect us" is how the next giant wins.</strong> Frost's research shows MNEs keep localising innovation. Without deliberately deepening local capability, your advantage decays — do the structural work now.`,
                    coach_r4_survival:      `<strong>A board-ready survival case.</strong> Share defended on non-transferable advantage, margin intact, a moat embedded to compound — that is a local champion built to meet the next entrant from strength, not a number that flatters one quarter.`,
                    coach_r4_pricewin:      `<strong>"We held volume by undercutting" is a price-war victory lap that hides the wound.</strong> Volume bought with margin against a USD 70B rival isn't a win — it's a slow bleed. The board should be asking how many quarters of this you can fund.`,
                    coach_r4_exit:          `<strong>Selling to Continental ends the story you could have won.</strong> The warung moat was the one asset the giant couldn't build — cashing out hands it over and dissolves the local champion. There was defensible ground here you chose not to hold.`,

